{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":26181250,"reputation":105,"user_id":514600,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/8MNZEGIT.png?s=256","display_name":"Number","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/514600/number"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1729635040,"post_id":434487,"comment_id":1252696,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Furthermore, <i>The Symmetries of Things</i> is loaded with beautiful full color illustrations (a few even similar to M.C. Escher&#39;s art), which make this book appear to be a coffee-table book, enticing unsuspecting non-mathematicians into mathematics."},{"owner":{"account_id":945133,"reputation":11410,"user_id":12178,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/6pgFY2BM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Tom Copeland","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/12178/tom-copeland"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1669836561,"post_id":323091,"comment_id":1122248,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A brief, very readable, introductory survey of basic complex algebra on par with some other books mentioned in this stream is &quot;The Skeleton Key of mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories&quot; by Littlewood, with short shrift on the underlying history of the ideas but with mention of applications to relativity and quantum mechanics."},{"owner":{"account_id":1737551,"reputation":295,"user_id":26085,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ed970a91837d68e82e80b6b5bb4872f8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joel Adler","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/26085/joel-adler"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1631351606,"post_id":322996,"comment_id":1033890,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"As I mentioned already earlier, I have read Villany&#39;s book and I suffered."},{"owner":{"account_id":180136,"reputation":37089,"user_id":2530,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":71,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/da66e743d724f074cc4a71248dedd19f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"darij grinberg","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/2530/darij-grinberg"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1631336483,"post_id":323039,"comment_id":1033866,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I remember trying to peruse Schroeder&#39;s book for an explanation of how he used quadratic residues to build diffusers. Maybe it&#39;s just me not understanding physics, but I still have no idea. My impression from some other sections of the book, though, is just that it is not well-written. E.g., can you tell from section 16.6 what the spirals on Figs. 16.1 and 16.2 have to do with the $S(m, k)$s?"},{"owner":{"account_id":945133,"reputation":11410,"user_id":12178,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/6pgFY2BM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Tom Copeland","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/12178/tom-copeland"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1631327791,"post_id":323059,"comment_id":1033853,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s admirable of him to provide free copies on the Net---worth a written thanks."},{"owner":{"account_id":510025,"reputation":7238,"user_id":3711,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6e4a9196c396df2326d05baceac2902f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"arsmath","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/3711/arsmath"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1590319842,"post_id":323006,"comment_id":910441,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I just started looking through that book.  Chapter 3 is amazing."},{"owner":{"account_id":2958032,"reputation":101111,"user_id":25510,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fz8IKyr6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Alexandre Eremenko","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/25510/alexandre-eremenko"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1575514641,"post_id":323025,"comment_id":870551,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for the recommendation of the book of Ghys. I&#39;d put in near the top of my list if I knew about it!"},{"owner":{"account_id":511230,"reputation":41599,"user_id":3684,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":88,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6073eed709acded7ca570cd86c80cd93?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Gerry Myerson","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/3684/gerry-myerson"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2958032,"reputation":101111,"user_id":25510,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fz8IKyr6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Alexandre Eremenko","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/25510/alexandre-eremenko"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1575514603,"post_id":322996,"comment_id":870550,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Alexandre, I read it, and enjoyed some of it."},{"owner":{"account_id":2958032,"reputation":101111,"user_id":25510,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fz8IKyr6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Alexandre Eremenko","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/25510/alexandre-eremenko"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1575514289,"post_id":322996,"comment_id":870548,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Have you read and enjoyed &quot;Birth of a theorem&quot;?"},{"owner":{"account_id":1520089,"reputation":9133,"user_id":13552,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":17,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/6b037872fe22f944c4af24fc1185f2bd?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Adrien","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/13552/adrien"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1557564394,"post_id":323027,"comment_id":826024,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"+1, and his book on Fermat last theorem is fantastic too, although they&#39;re maybe more elementary than what OP had in mind."},{"owner":{"account_id":945133,"reputation":11410,"user_id":12178,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":60,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/6pgFY2BM.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Tom Copeland","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/12178/tom-copeland"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1550488624,"post_id":323091,"comment_id":806990,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A little more advanced, yet also presented with perceptive intuitions and illustrations, the books noted in <a href=\"https://mathoverflow.net/questions/31879/are-there-other-nice-math-books-close-to-the-style-of-tristan-needham/102712#102712\" title=\"are there other nice math books close to the style of tristan needham\">mathoverflow.net/questions/31879/&hellip;</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":9143205,"reputation":159,"user_id":135728,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/16717730289b7be3b0b7deb87962228f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Moritz Raguschat","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/135728/moritz-raguschat"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":10780809,"reputation":3365,"user_id":109085,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Z1z8R.jpg?s=256","display_name":"efs","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/109085/efs"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1550329515,"post_id":323059,"comment_id":806733,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Okay, &quot;casual&quot; is probably not the right word, at least for the middle and later parts of the book. But I think for the beginning chapters it might be applicable. And when one&#39;s interest has been captivated, the following suffering is ameliorated by a sense of purpose.  Hatcher has retired from teaching I think many years ago, but is still active in research and writing. It states so on his uni web page: <a href=\"https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher</a> But I guess it&#39;s not to be expected then that his public works would disappear from the web anyway."},{"owner":{"account_id":2958032,"reputation":101111,"user_id":25510,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fz8IKyr6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Alexandre Eremenko","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/25510/alexandre-eremenko"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1550081978,"post_id":323016,"comment_id":806187,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The title is &quot;Gamma. Exploring Euler&#39;s constant&quot;."},{"owner":{"account_id":10780809,"reputation":3365,"user_id":109085,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Z1z8R.jpg?s=256","display_name":"efs","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/109085/efs"},"edited":false,"score":19,"creation_date":1549986392,"post_id":323059,"comment_id":805970,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I would not classify AT as a &quot;casual&quot; reading. I actually suffered reading that book when I took a course in AT. Also, he published in 2017, so I do not think he is retired."},{"owner":{"account_id":10780809,"reputation":3365,"user_id":109085,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Z1z8R.jpg?s=256","display_name":"efs","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/109085/efs"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1549985933,"post_id":323026,"comment_id":805968,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Narkiewicz has just published &quot;The Story of Algebraic Numbers in the First Half of the 20th Century From Hilbert to Tate&quot;."},{"owner":{"account_id":2958032,"reputation":101111,"user_id":25510,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fz8IKyr6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Alexandre Eremenko","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/25510/alexandre-eremenko"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2958470,"reputation":5775,"user_id":54780,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":37,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f29e8cbad183330689cbcc211c3a2d25?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Alex M.","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/54780/alex-m"},"edited":false,"score":7,"creation_date":1549941871,"post_id":322995,"comment_id":805884,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Alex M. It is not about Galois theory. It is about Galois dream. About the theory he wanted to create (as we know from his letters)."},{"owner":{"account_id":2958032,"reputation":101111,"user_id":25510,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fz8IKyr6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Alexandre Eremenko","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/25510/alexandre-eremenko"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1549941727,"post_id":323016,"comment_id":805883,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Many Carus monographs are excellent. MY favorite one is P. Doyle and L. Snell, Random walks and electric networks."},{"owner":{"account_id":2958032,"reputation":101111,"user_id":25510,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fz8IKyr6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Alexandre Eremenko","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/25510/alexandre-eremenko"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2960220,"reputation":2671,"user_id":29316,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":62,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/uEZyO.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amir Asghari","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/29316/amir-asghari"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1549940329,"post_id":322995,"comment_id":805881,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Amir Asghari: thanks for the correction. I only have the Russian original."},{"owner":{"account_id":2958470,"reputation":5775,"user_id":54780,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":37,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f29e8cbad183330689cbcc211c3a2d25?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Alex M.","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/54780/alex-m"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1549922877,"post_id":322995,"comment_id":805844,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"To the reader: the title of Kuga&#39;s book might be misleading: the book is not about classical Galois theory (as one might expect), but about some of its more recent extensions, such as fundamental groups of covering spaces or differential Galois theory."},{"owner":{"account_id":2960220,"reputation":2671,"user_id":29316,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":62,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/uEZyO.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amir Asghari","link":"https://mathoverflow.net/users/29316/amir-asghari"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1549922695,"post_id":322995,"comment_id":805843,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Kirillov&#39;s is &quot;What are numbers?&quot;"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":296}